# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Arrays", worked example "Writing at one position and reading at another".
id: array-aliasing
title: Writing at one position and reading at another
topic: Arrays
level: 3
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Arrays, "Writing at one position and reading at another"
tags: arrays, array assignment, aliasing, select-store

task:
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the statement for the
  postcondition $Q$ with the rule ARRAY-ASSIGN, in three steps:
  substitution, select-store, and definedness. Then use the consequence
  rule to decide whether the triple is valid.

  Before calculating, think: what happens if $i = j$? And if $i != j$,
  what must be true about $a[j]$?

pre: (j = i || a[j] = 0) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= j < sizeof(a)
post: a[i] = a[j]

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  a[i] := 2 * a[j]     // i1
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of a[i] := 2 * a[j]
rule: ARRAY-ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  The statement has the shape $a[I] := E$ with $I = i$ and
  $E = 2 * a[j]$. ARRAY-ASSIGN substitutes the array $a$ in $Q$ with the
  updated array $a[i |-> 2 * a[j]]$. $Q$ reads the array twice, so both
  reads refer to the updated array. The rule adds $def(a[i])$ for the
  write and $def(a[j])$ for the read in $E$.
calc:
  wp(a[i] := 2 * a[j], Q)
  = (a[i] = a[j])[a <- a[i |-> 2 * a[j]]] && def(a[i]) && def(a[j])
  = a[i |-> 2 * a[j]][i] = a[i |-> 2 * a[j]][j] && def(a[i]) && def(a[j])     // both a of Q are replaced by a[i |-> 2 * a[j]]
  = 2 * a[j] = a[i |-> 2 * a[j]][j] && def(a[i]) && def(a[j])     // select-store, left read: index i is the written index, so it gives 2 * a[j]
  = ((j = i && 2 * a[j] = 2 * a[j]) || (j != i && 2 * a[j] = a[j])) && def(a[i]) && def(a[j])     // select-store, right read: j may or may not be i, so we keep both cases
  = (j = i || (j != i && 2 * a[j] = a[j])) && def(a[i]) && def(a[j])     // 2 * a[j] = 2 * a[j] is true, and A && true is A
  = (j = i || (j != i && a[j] = 0)) && def(a[i]) && def(a[j])     // subtract a[j] from both sides of 2 * a[j] = a[j]
  = (j = i || a[j] = 0) && def(a[i]) && def(a[j])     // A || (!A && B) is A || B
  = (j = i || a[j] = 0) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= j < sizeof(a)     // def(a[J]) is 0 <= J < sizeof(a)
set: R1 = (j = i || a[j] = 0) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= j < sizeof(a)
note:
  The brackets around $j = i || a[j] = 0$ matter: the definedness
  conditions must hold in both cases.

step: The precondition must imply the weakest precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE
text:
  $R1$ is the weakest precondition of the statement for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R1$.
vc: (j = i || a[j] = 0) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= j < sizeof(a) ==> (j = i || a[j] = 0) && 0 <= i < sizeof(a) && 0 <= j < sizeof(a)
why:
  $P$ and $R1$ are the same formula.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  When $i = j$, $a[i]$ and $a[j]$ are the same cell, so $Q$ holds whatever
  value is written. When $i != j$, after the write $a[i]$ is $2 * a[j]$
  and $a[j]$ keeps its value, so they are equal only when $a[j] = 0$. A
  test, each time from the array $4, 0, 9$:

  | $i$ | $j$ | the case that holds | array after | $a[i] = a[j]$ |
  |---|---|---|---|---|
  | $0$ | $0$ | $j = i$ | $8, 0, 9$ | $8 = 8$, true |
  | $0$ | $1$ | $a[1] = 0$ | $0, 0, 9$ | $0 = 0$, true |
  | $0$ | $2$ | none | $18, 0, 9$ | $18 = 9$, false |
